{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>YHWH possessed me, the beginning of His way, the first of His works of old (Proverbs 8:22).</b>" "<b>Solomon</b> the king, peace be upon him, speaks in this verse in the name of Wisdom — that is, the hidden Wisdom, concealed even from the ministering angels, as it is written (Job 28:21): ״והחכמה מאין תמצא״, and it is written (ibid.): ״ונעלמה מעיני כל חי ומעוף השמים נסתרה״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: ״ומעוף השמים״ — these are the ministering angels. And it is the Wisdom that preceded the act of Creation, as it is written (Proverbs 3:19–20): ״YHWH בחכמה יסד ארץ כונן שמים בתבונה״, and it is written (ibid.): ״בדעתו תהומות נבקעו״ — teaching you that the heavens and the earth and the deeps and all that is in them, which are the totality of all existents, above and below — all were created through the power of Wisdom. And this is what is mentioned here (Proverbs 8:24 ff.): ״באין תהומות חוללתי באין מעינות נכבדי מים בטרם הרים הטבעו״ and so on, ״לא עשה ארץ וחוצות״; and in the inner meaning of the matter, תהומות is the green line, והרים are the patriarchs, and ארץ is the sefirah of Malkhut. And this Wisdom Solomon called ראשית, because it preceded the act of Creation, and from this the matter was compelling and it was necessary to begin the act of Creation with the word בראשית. And the term ראשית embraces precedence in time and also embraces precedence of rank, from the language of (Amos 6:6): ״וראשית שמנים ימשחו״, which is the choicest of oils; for just as oil rises above all other liquids, so Wisdom is above all existents. And this is what it says: ״YHWH קנני ראשית דרכו״ — Wisdom says that it is ראשית: ״YHWH קנני״, that is, the supreme hidden Source brought me into being, for He is the first Cause, and He is the source of Wisdom, from which Wisdom is found and drawn, and He alone is the Source. And the word קנני is from the language of קן (nest), which is the first foundation in the coming-into-being of birds; and he explains here that the Source is the cause of Wisdom, which is the first nest, and Wisdom, which is the nest, is the cause of all products, just as the nest is the cause of the chicks. And this verse reveals to you that all products exist through the cause of Wisdom, which is ראשית, and Wisdom exists through the cause of the supreme hidden Source; and if so, Wisdom is not created from nothing and naught, as some of the philosophers hold, but rather it is found and drawn from אין, which is the supreme hidden Source. And this is the meaning of Job's statement, who revealed this matter to us, saying (Job 28:12): ״והחכמה מאין תמצא״ — and this is a way of declaration, not a way of questioning. Now, our holy Torah emanates and is drawn from this Wisdom, and because it is taken (לקוח) from it, it is called לקח טוב, and over it the ministering angels were envious. And so they said in the Midrash: When the Holy One, blessed be He, said that He would give the Torah to Israel at Mount Sinai, the ministering angels were envious and said before Him: Master of the Universe! A treasured possession that You have in heaven — You are giving it to human beings, who possess impurity, who possess the evil inclination! As it is written (Psalms 8:2): ״YHWH אדונינו מה אדיר שמך בכל הארץ אשר תנה הודך על השמים״ — do not set Your splendor upon the earth, but in heaven! The Holy One, blessed be He, said to them: The Torah that I am giving to human beings — is written in it: ״זכור את יום השבת לקדשו״ — do you have labor among you? ״כבד את אביך ואת אמך״ — do you have a father and mother? ״לא תרצח לא תנאף לא תגנוב״ — is there an evil inclination among you? ״את זה תאכלו ואת זה לא תאכלו״ — is there eating and drinking among you? — thus far this Midrash." "<b>According to</b> my opinion, the expression נתקנאו (they were envious) is because the angels have no envy among them, for envy is among the traits of the evil inclination, and the angels have no evil inclination. And so they said: Above there is no sitting, no standing, no envy, and no rivalry, because all of them concur in a single aim, which is the apprehension of the Creator, blessed be He, and this apprehension is the sustenance and existence of the ministering angels. And just as a man is drawn after bodily food, which is his existence, so the angels are drawn after their sustenance, which is the apprehension of Him, blessed be He, and that is their existence. And since they have only one will and one understanding, it is impossible for envy to be found among them. And it is further well known that the envy found in the human species exists only among those who are close in rank, for the king is not envious of the villager, and the villager is not envious of the king, because they are at the utmost distance from one another. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory said: A person is envious only of a sage against a sage, a mighty man against a mighty man, a rich man against a rich man. And it is clear that the sage is not envious of the fool, for he has no need of him; and the fool is not envious of the sage, for he has no understanding at all such that he could recognize his own worth and the worth of the sage, for the two are opposites — and if so, how could one thing be envious of its opposite, and one who is supreme in rank, how could he be envious of one who is lowly in rank? But the matter of envy among the angels is a metaphor, and the intent is that it was far and impossible according to the intellect of the ministering angels, that the Wisdom which is hidden from them — given that they are simple intellect and have no evil inclination — the Holy One, blessed be He, would give a Torah drawn from it to possessors of an evil inclination. From this it was said: the ministering angels were envious, and they said: A treasured possession that You have in heaven — You are giving it to human beings! For they certainly knew that all these matters are mentioned in the Torah, and that they are physical commandments impossible to fulfill except by possessors of a body. And the reason for the envy is the matter of preventiveness on their part, that there should be elevation of rank for composite possessors of matter above the separate intellects free of matter, and also the elevation of rank that belonged to Moses our master, peace be upon him, that he should apprehend the Wisdom that is hidden from them. This rank called ראשית no created being attained as did Moses our master; and concerning this the verse testifies about him (Deuteronomy 33:21): ״וירא ראשית לו״ — for there, in the rank of ראשית, ״חלקת מחוקק ספון״, and from there he merited to receive the Torah. And this apprehension of his was not made known to him through prophecy, but only from the side of his contemplation in Wisdom; and this is the language of וירא (he saw), and it does not say וידע (he knew). But the rank that our Rabbis of blessed memory called ״the clear mirror״ (אספקלריא המאירה), and which is the attribute of mercy, was made known to him through prophecy — unlike the patriarchs of the world and all the rest of the prophets, who knew YHWH, blessed be He, through a revelation of the Shekhinah that appeared to them in prophecy; but the unique Name (ה׳ המיוחד) was not made known to them through prophecy. And this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded in tractate Yevamot at the end of the chapter החולץ: What is the difference between the prophecy of Moses and the prophecy of all the other prophets? Moses contemplated באספקלריא המאירה, and the rest of the prophets did not contemplate באספקלריא המאירה. He called the rank from which Moses prophesied ״the clear mirror״ (אספקלריא המאירה), and it is the attribute through which public miracles, renewed in nature, are performed; and the mirror (אספקלריא) of El Shaddai, through which hidden miracles are performed, he called ״the mirror that does not illuminate״ (אספקלריא שאינה מאירה), and it is the apprehension of the patriarchs. And the word אספקלריא is a notarikon: ספק קלריא, that is, an effusion of light, and it is a clear, brilliant manifestation like the case of pure and shining glass, through which a person sees from outside what is within it; and the Targum of (Job 28:17) ״לא יערכנה זהב וזכוכית״ is ״דהבא ואספקלריא״. And what they said ״that does not illuminate״ — the intent is not that the effusion of light would not be great and mighty, for it is indeed an אספקלריא; rather, the intent is to say that it does not give light to the one who contemplates in it, unlike the rank of Moses, which was אספקלריא המאירה, which is with a doubled and redoubled effusion of light and gives light to the one who contemplates in it — and therefore the skin of his face radiated. And therefore you will not find that the verses mention the unique Name in connection with Abraham, but only with אל״ף דל״ת, or אל״ף דל״ת alone; whereas with Moses the verse does not mention שדי, but only ה׳ המיוחד. And these two apprehensions — the apprehension of the patriarchs and the apprehension of Moses, from which Moses prophesied — these are the two mirrors; the unique Name and El Shaddai are the difference between the prophecy of Moses and the prophecy of the patriarchs. And this is what is written:" "<b>And I appeared to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob, as El Shaddai, but My name YHWH I did not make known to them.</b> He used the expression וידבר אלהים and did not say ויאמר ה׳, because the language of דבור (speaking) is harsh language, as it is written (Genesis 42:30): ״דבר האיש אדוני הארץ אתנו קשות״. And we find in the section of the Burning Bush sixteen utterances (אמירות) to Moses from the mouth of the Almighty, and in none of them is דבור mentioned; but here, because Moses said ״למה הרעותה״, He used the expression וידבר. And the term אלהים is also the attribute of judgment (דין), and it comes to reprove him and to speak judgment with him over this. ״ויאמר אליו אני ה׳״ — faithful to exact punishment; and when it is said concerning reward, its meaning is: faithful to pay reward, as it is written (Leviticus 19:3): ״איש אמו ואביו תיראו ואת שבתותי תשמרו אני ה׳״ — faithful to pay reward; and when it is said concerning punishment, such as (ibid.): ״וחללת את שם אלהיך אני ה׳״ — its meaning is: faithful to exact punishment. Now, the Holy One, blessed be He, brought him a proof in this reproof from the patriarchs, who did not attain the level of prophecy in as great a rank as he did, and yet did not say this to Him and did not question His ways. And concerning this He said: ״וארא״ — I appeared to the patriarchs as El Shaddai, that is, with that power which overrides the celestial influences; but ״ושמי ה׳ לא נודעתי להם״ — by My unique Name, through which all that exists comes into being, I did not make Myself known to them, to create for them novelties through public miracles involving a change in nature. And according to how He began with ״וארא״, He should have said ״ושמי ה׳ לא נראיתי״, or one might say: ״ואודע אל אברהם אל יצחק ואל יעקב ושמי ה׳ לא נודעתי להם״; but because the prophecy of the patriarchs came through night visions, He needed to say ״וארא״, and the prophecy of Moses, which was face to face, required saying ״לא נודעתי להם״ — as I made Myself known to you. And from the fact that He used the expression נודעתי and did not say הודעתי, He comes to teach that He and His Name are one; and so we find (Exodus 33:22): ״והיה בעבור כבודי״, and He said (ibid.): ״עד עברי״ — He did not say ״עד עבור כבודי״, but rather ״עד עברי״; from here, that He and His glory are one." "<b>And the Ramban,</b> of blessed memory, wrote: And by way of truth, the verse comes according to its plain sense and its implication — ״I am YHWH: I appeared to them in the mirror of El Shaddai,״ in the sense of (Numbers 12:6): ״במראה אליו אתודע״; ״ושמי ה׳ לא נודעתי להם״ — they did not contemplate in the clear mirror (אספקלריא המאירה), that they should know Me in the sense of knowing YHWH face to face. For the patriarchs knew the unique Name YHWH, but it was not made known to them through prophecy; and therefore, when the verse mentions Abraham, it mentions the unique Name with אל״ף דל״ת, or אל״ף דל״ת alone. Now, the matter is that the patriarchs had revelation of the Shekhinah and discourse with it in the weak attribute of judgment (דין), and He conducted Himself with them through it; whereas with Moses, He will conduct Himself and make Himself known through the attribute of mercy, which is His great Name, in the sense of (Isaiah 63:12): ״מוליך לימין משה זרוע תפארתו״, and it is written (ibid.): ״כן נהגת עמך לעשות לך שם תפארת״. And therefore Moses will not be mentioned henceforth with the name El Shaddai, for the Torah was given through His great Name, as it says: ״אנכי ה׳ אלהיך״, and as it says (Deuteronomy 4:36): ״מן השמים השמיעך את קולו ליסרך ועל הארץ הראך את אשו הגדולה״ — and I have already hinted at the interpretation of השמים, and may the Holy One, blessed be He, open our eyes and show us wonders from His Torah. And the sense of ״וגם הקימותי את בריתי אתם וגם אני שמעתי״ is: I appeared to them as El Shaddai, and also I established for them that covenant before Me; and also, through My great Name, I have now heard the groan of the children of Israel, and I will remember My covenant which I established with them with Me. And the discerning will understand — thus far the language of the Rabbi, of blessed memory."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :register {:flags {3 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:57:10.588489930Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}